Here is the full **testsmells.org 19-smell academic audit** of `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 1           | 1              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4**          |

**Calibration note:** This class is marked `[TestCategory("Integration")]`. Per integration-test calibration rules, Mystery Guest and Eager Test severity would be downgraded — but neither was detected here. The SQLite in-memory database is a hermetic test resource, not a mystery guest. No test calls 4+ distinct production methods. The 4 findings below are genuine smells that apply regardless of integration scope.

---

## Findings by Severity

### ❶ Sleepy Test — HIGH

**Smell:** Sleepy Test (testsmells.org core)
**Method:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 82–90)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);  // ← blocks for 3 seconds, non-deterministic
```

**Why it's a problem:** Adds 3 seconds to every test run. If the system is slower than expected, the test becomes flaky; if faster, it wastes time. This smell correlates with flaky test suites (Camara et al., SAST 2021). Even for integration tests, `Thread.Sleep` is a smell — use event-driven waiting instead.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    // Poll with timeout instead of fixed sleep
    var notified = await WaitUntilAsync(
        () => _repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        timeout: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

    Assert.IsTrue(notified, "Notification was not sent within timeout.");
}

private static async Task<bool> WaitUntilAsync(Func<bool> condition, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow + timeout;
    while (DateTime.UtcNow < deadline)
    {
        if (condition()) return true;
        await Task.Delay(50);
    }
    return false;
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky CI failures; suite slowdown compounds as more sleepy tests are added.

---

### ❷ Conditional Test Logic — HIGH

**Smell:** Conditional Test Logic (testsmells.org core)
**Method:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 94–109)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);   // path A
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);       // path B
}
```

**Why it's a problem:** Only one branch executes per run. If the repository always returns a base `User`, the `PremiumUser` branch is dead code — you have hidden testing gaps. The test never proves which path *should* be taken.

**Fix:** Split into two explicit tests with deterministic setup:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandardUser_ReturnsUserWithCorrectName()
{
    var user = new User("eve@example.com", "Eve");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscountRate()
{
    var user = new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m);
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com") as PremiumUser;

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** One branch is silently untested; bugs in the dead path go undetected.

---

### ❸ Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test) — HIGH

**Smell:** Unknown Test / Assertion-Free Test (testsmells.org core)
**Method:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113–119)

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← No assertions — passes even if every insert silently fails
}
```

**Why it's a problem:** MSTest marks this as ✅ passed as long as no exception is thrown. If `Insert` silently swallows errors or inserts corrupted data, this test provides false confidence. The name says "runs without errors" but it doesn't verify the result.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count, "Expected all 100 bulk-inserted users to be persisted.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False pass — coverage looks good but nothing is verified.

---

### ❹ Assertion Roulette — MEDIUM

**Smell:** Assertion Roulette (testsmells.org extended catalog)
**Method:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 28–39)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);           // which failed?
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);   // this one?
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);  // or this?
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);       // or this?
```

4 assertions, zero message parameters. When one fails in CI, the developer must open the source to figure out which property was wrong.

**Fix — add message parameters:**
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert.");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip.");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip.");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Id should be auto-assigned a positive value.");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower failure diagnosis, especially in CI logs where you can't click through to the line.

---

## Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The remaining **4 tests** are well-structured:

| Test | Why it's good |
|------|---------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange-act-assert, 2 focused assertions |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Single assertion verifying absence — clean |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | `Assert.AreEqual(3, ...)` is contextually obvious (3 items just added) — not a Magic Number smell |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Good concurrency test with proper `await` and clear assertion |

The `[TestInitialize]`/`[TestCleanup]` setup is also clean: both `_connection` and `_repository` are used by every test method — no **General Fixture** smell. Using in-memory SQLite avoids the **Mystery Guest** smell entirely.

---

## Smells Not Detected (15 of 19)

Explicitly checked and clean: Mystery Guest, Eager Test, Magic Number Test, Sensitive Equality, Exception Handling, General Fixture, Ignored Test, Duplicate Assert, Lazy Test, Constructor Initialization, Default Test, Redundant Print, Redundant Assertion, Resource Optimism, Empty Test.

---

## Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Effort |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| 1 | **Assertion-Free Test** | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, ...)` after bulk insert | 2 min |
| 2 | **Conditional Test Logic** | Split into 2 deterministic tests | 10 min |
| 3 | **Sleepy Test** | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with poll-with-timeout helper | 15 min |
| 4 | **Assertion Roulette** | Add message strings to 4 assertions | 3 min |

Fix **#1** first — it's the highest-risk smell (false pass) with the lowest effort (one line). Fixes #2 and #3 eliminate the two flakiness vectors. Fix #4 is cosmetic but improves CI debuggability.
